# ET COMPRESSOR v3.0.0

## Master Improvement Plan — From Adequate to Exceptional

`PDT ∘ SOVEREIGN ∘ MANIFOLD`

---

## Section 0 — EXECUTIVE DIAGNOSIS: Why v2.1.0 Underperforms

The ET Compressor v2.1.0 has sound ET-grounded architecture but its compression pipeline suffers from **seven critical bottlenecks** that prevent it from approaching theoretical limits. The scanner results prove that **compression = T-extraction** (Traverser removal) — the compressor must aggressively extract indeterminate forms and replace them with minimal descriptor representations. Currently it does this at only a surface level.

> **Critical Issue #1 — Huffman Ceiling**
>
> *Entropy coding is capped at integer bit boundaries*
>
> Huffman coding assigns whole-bit codes to symbols. For a symbol with probability 0.3, the optimal code length is ~1.74 bits, but Huffman must assign 2 bits — a 15% waste on *every symbol*. Modern compressors (zstd, brotli, lzma) use Arithmetic Coding or ANS (Asymmetric Numeral Systems) which encode fractional bits, approaching the Shannon entropy limit. This single change alone can improve ratios by 5–15%.

> **Critical Issue #2 — Zero Context Modeling**
>
> *No prediction, no context mixing, no probability estimation*
>
> The compressor has no model of what byte is likely to appear next. Modern compressors maintain multi-order context models (PPM, LZMA's Markov chains, PAQ-style context mixing). Without prediction, the compressor cannot exploit the dominant source of redundancy in real data: **sequential dependencies**. The D-field gradient and curvature analysis exists but is used only for strategy *selection*, not for actual prediction-driven compression.

> **Critical Issue #3 — Weak LZ77 Implementation**
>
> *No lazy matching, no optimal parsing, limited window*
>
> The LZ77 engine uses a basic trigram hash with a 32KB window and 64-candidate chain. It lacks: lazy matching (checking if the next position yields a longer match), optimal parsing (choosing globally optimal match/literal decisions), larger window sizes (zstd uses 128KB–1MB+), and match-length encoding efficiency. The literal encoding uses 2 bytes per literal (flag + byte) — a 100% overhead on unmatchable data.

> **Critical Issue #4 — Naive BWT Sort (O(n²))**
>
> *BWT uses Python list.sort on doubled data — catastrophically slow and memory-heavy*
>
> The BWT doubles the input data and sorts index arrays by comparing full rotations. This is O(n² log n) in the worst case and uses 2× memory. Modern BWT implementations use SA-IS (Suffix Array Induced Sorting) which is O(n) time and O(n) memory. For a 64KB block, the current BWT creates a 128KB doubled buffer and performs millions of string comparisons. This makes BWT blocks lose the competitive compression race even when they'd produce better results.

> **Critical Issue #5 — Single-Pass Block Independence**
>
> *No cross-block learning, no dictionary sharing, no second-pass refinement*
>
> Each block is compressed independently with zero knowledge of other blocks. Patterns that repeat across blocks are re-encoded from scratch. There is no shared dictionary, no inter-block reference chain, and no multi-pass optimization. The deduplication engine exists but is never used in the competitive compression path.

> **Critical Issue #6 — Python Inner Loop Speed**
>
> *Sovereign's power is barely utilized — only memmove is used*
>
> The Sovereign integration is limited to `ctypes.memmove` for memory copies. The actual compression inner loops (LZ77 matching, BWT sorting, Huffman encoding, MTF transform) all run in pure Python at ~100× slower than C. Sovereign provides **assembly JIT execution, executable memory allocation, and function hot-swapping** — none of which are used for the performance-critical paths.

> **Critical Issue #7 — Descriptor Residual Underuse**
>
> *Only triggers for blocks ≤ 4096 bytes, residuals not entropy-coded*
>
> The descriptor-residual strategy (Eq 233) is limited to small blocks and its residual stream uses zigzag varint encoding without entropy coding. The residuals after function fitting are often highly compressible (clustered near zero), but without Huffman/ANS on the residual stream, this potential is wasted. Additionally, the polynomial search should try more basis functions (Fourier terms, wavelets, piecewise linear).

