## Section 5 — SA-IS BWT WITH SOVEREIGN JIT `HIGH`

Replace the naive O(n²) BWT with O(n) SA-IS (Suffix Array by Induced Sorting). This is essential because the BWT strategy currently **loses the competitive race** due to speed, even when it would produce the best compression ratio. SA-IS + Sovereign JIT makes BWT competitive on all block sizes.

### Changes to `_bwt_encode` / `_bwt_decode`

Complete replacement of the sort algorithm:

- **SA-IS implementation:** O(n) suffix array construction. Classifies each suffix as S-type or L-type, identifies LMS (leftmost S-type) suffixes as structural breakpoints (Eq 239), sorts LMS suffixes by induction, then induces the full suffix array.
- **BWT from SA:** Given suffix array SA, BWT[i] = data[(SA[i] - 1) mod n]. O(n) extraction.
- **Inverse BWT:** Use the standard LF-mapping algorithm (O(n) time, O(n) space) instead of the current simple table-based approach.
- **Sovereign JIT acceleration:** The SA-IS inner loop (bucket sorting, induction steps) is the hottest path. Compile to x86-64 assembly via Sovereign's `allocate_executable` + `ctypes.CFUNCTYPE` for 10-50× speedup on large blocks.
- **Block size increase:** With O(n) sort, increase `BWT_MAX_BLOCK` from 64KB to 1MB+ — larger BWT blocks compress significantly better.

### Eq 239 Required — Suffix Array Manifold Ordering

```
SA = induced_sort(LMS_sorted(data))   where   LMS = breakpoints(D-field)
```

SA-IS derived as manifold-ordered traversal. LMS suffixes ↔ descriptor field breakpoints (Eq 135 classification), induction ↔ recursive discovery (Eq 217).

